I managed to pinpoint the cause of the issue. It seems that `zone.js` is not interacting well with `undici`, the internal library used by Node.js for handling `fetch` operations.

I confirmed this with the following code:

```js
import "zone.js/node";
import { fetch } from "undici";

(async () => {
  for (let i = 0; i <= 100; i++) {
    console.log(i + 1);
    await fetch("https://reqres.in/api/users");
  }

  setTimeout(() => {}, 50_000);
})();
```

In the memory snapshot, we can observe 153 instances of `Timeout`:
<img width="833" alt="Screenshot 2024-07-03 at 09 17 17" src="https://github.com/angular/angular/assets/17563226/b2a680b9-1c7b-4e41-b8c5-d4b87c9f5c3f">

However, removing `zone.js` results in no additional `Timeout` instances:
<img width="833" alt="Screenshot 2024-07-03 at 09 22 14" src="https://github.com/angular/angular/assets/17563226/20fadde4-5514-4906-90ed-fedfad7ef4fb">

---

**Edit:** Upon further investigation, it seems that the issue arises when `Timeout.refresh()` is called in https://github.com/nodejs/undici/blob/1dff4fd9b1b2cee97c5f8cf44041521a62d3f133/lib/util/timers.js#L45
